# Repository Guidelines

## Project Structure & Module Organization

IMSWeb is a pnpm monorepo with two application workspaces and one shared contracts package:

- `apps/api/`: Hono backend for Node; source is in `src/` and tests in `tests/`.
- `apps/web/`: React Router 7 frontend; the typed route manifest lives in `app/routes.ts` and points directly to route-ready page modules in `app/pages/`; layouts live in `app/layouts/`, shared UI in `app/components/`, API contracts and clients in `app/lib/api/`, and assets in `public/`.
- `packages/contracts/`: @imsweb/contracts workspace package holding cross-workspace wire-format contracts (zod schemas as the single source of truth; both apps depend on it via workspace:*).
- `data/`: ignored local databases, uploads, and migration inputs; only `.gitignore` is tracked.

Migration, deployment, and boundary tooling lives in `scripts/`, `deploy/`, `docs/`, and root `tests/`. Keep dependencies inside their owning workspace.

## Build, Test, and Development Commands

Use Node.js `>=22.13.0` and pnpm 11. Install once from the repository root:

```sh
corepack enable
pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
```

AI coding agents must follow `docs/development/ai-environment.md` for workspace
preflight, local data configuration, service startup, and validation evidence.

- `pnpm dev`: start PostgreSQL, Valkey, RustFS, migrations, Hono, and Web with hot reload.
- `pnpm run dev:doctor`: diagnose local prerequisites without changing state.
- `pnpm run dev:down`: stop local data services without deleting their volumes.
- `pnpm run dev:node`: watch only the Hono Node server.
- `pnpm run dev:web`: start only the Web dev server.
- `pnpm run build`: build API and Web after checking workspace boundaries.
- `pnpm run check`: run static, architecture, migration, lint, test, and build checks.
- `pnpm run check:pre-commit`: run the fast checks enforced by the Git pre-commit hook.
- `pnpm run test`: run infrastructure, API, Web unit, and routing-contract tests.
- `pnpm run test:web`: run Web unit and Playwright tests.
- `pnpm run test:web-routing`: validate the built Web client against Hono route ownership.

## Coding Style & Naming Conventions

Use strict TypeScript. API code uses four spaces, semicolons, single quotes, and the `@/` alias rooted at `apps/api/src`; Web and contracts code use their local `.rules` formatting conventions. New or substantially edited API files must follow the API style; do not create unrelated formatter churn solely to normalize historical files. Run `pnpm --filter @imsweb/web format` and `pnpm --filter @imsweb/web lint` for frontend changes. Use kebab-case filenames and PascalCase React components.

## Testing Guidelines

Tests use Node's runner, Vitest, Testing Library, Playwright, and Python `unittest`. Name tests `*.test.ts`, `*.test.tsx`, `*.test.js`, or `test_*.py`; E2E files use `*.spec.ts`. Add regressions beside the affected workspace. No numeric coverage threshold exists; API persistence changes must cover the active Node runtime.

## Documentation

Repository-level documentation lives under `docs/` and follows [`docs/README.md`](docs/README.md):
architecture, development, operations, migrations, governance, and archive are separate scopes. Every
`docs/**/*.md` file has the required metadata header, valid relative links, and an explicit authority
source. Run `pnpm run check:rules` to run the documentation checker as well as agent/source rules.
Workspace-specific behavior stays in the owning workspace README, DESIGN, or `.rules`; do not duplicate
wire contracts, path prefixes, migration ledgers, or deployment shell flows in prose.

## Wire Contracts (@imsweb/contracts)

`packages/contracts` zod schemas are the single source of truth for API↔Web wire
formats; static types derive via `z.infer`, and `apps/api/src/contracts` is
forbidden. The package is flat-first: one file per business domain, promoted to a
folder when a domain gains a second file, with `exports` subpaths mirroring file
paths and the root index exposing only camelCase namespaces (no flat
re-exports). Shared atom schemas export only from their owning core module;
sibling-internal atoms stay unexported to keep barrels ambiguity-free. Admin
schemas take an `admin` prefix and multi-source domains order public before
admin. Wire types (`z.infer` aliases and their indexed derivations) live beside
their schemas in the package; Web keeps only UI-semantic aliases, request/input
types, and `File`/`FormData` shapes. zod itself is encapsulated by the package: import `z` from
`@imsweb/contracts/z`; only `packages/contracts` may declare the zod dependency.
Cross-domain response commons (success envelope, cursor/snapshot/numbered
page-info) live in `@imsweb/contracts/common` and domain schemas compose them
via `successEnvelope`/shared refs instead of redefining inline. Shared API and
public delivery URL prefixes live in `@imsweb/contracts/paths`; both API route
registration/middleware and Web endpoints must use its path builders, keeping
only business-specific suffixes local. `pnpm run check:rules` runs the source
boundary checker for forbidden directories, direct zod imports, domain-to-infra
imports, Web test placement, and raw shared URL prefixes. API response
modules must not hand-write wire shapes: they alias package types (output
types, or `z.input` variants where schemas transform/coerce) and view builders
annotate returns with contract types; only API-local boundaries (media/site
streams, redirects, error bodies) stay local. API code imports contract types
as type-only so zod never loads in production runtime; runtime conformance is enforced by
`apps/api/tests/wiki/wire-contract-conformance.test.ts` and inline
`schema.parse` calls at HTTP test response read points.

## Commit & Pull Request Guidelines

History is short, but the latest commit uses Conventional Commit style (`feat: ...`). Prefer `feat:`, `fix:`, `test:`, or `docs:` with an imperative summary. PRs should name affected workspaces, describe migrations or configuration changes, list validation commands, link issues, and include screenshots for visible Web changes.

## Security & Configuration

Copy settings from the owner-specific templates in `apps/api/.env.example`,
`apps/web/.env.example`, and `deploy/.env.example`; never commit secrets, databases, uploads, or
generated build output. Production requires distinct high-entropy
`IMS_BACKOFFICE_JWT_SECRET` and `IMS_PLATFORM_JWT_SECRET` values; `IMS_JWT_SECRET` is only the
temporary legacy Backoffice verification secret.
PostgreSQL migration work must keep credentials outside the repository and preserve the existing Core/Story data reconciliation gates. Historical Express/Flask source, data, and assets belong to a separate private repository and must not be copied into this public tree.
